 ## Rules
 
 - Never print, log, summarize, or repeat secret values. Pass `op://` references around instead.
-- Any 1Password call may show the user an approval prompt, and each new shell you open can need a fresh approval. Batch a task's work into as few commands as possible: many queries in one `find`, many references in one env file run with `env run`.
-- Never run `op whoami` or any other preflight check. Just run the command; the desktop app authorizes it when needed.
+- Service-account access stays within its granted vaults. Desktop authentication may show an approval prompt, and each new shell can need a fresh approval. Batch authorized work: many queries in one `find`, many references in one env file run with `env run`.
+- Never run `op whoami` or authentication preflight calls. Run the authorized command with the existing agent configuration; use the recovery procedure below if its execution boundary blocks it.
 - Never retry a write that failed or reported "unverified". Run `find` first to check whether it already happened.
+
+## Use the existing agent configuration
+
+Start with the configured service account and its existing vault scope. Do not replace it, reconnect it, or run setup because one command failed. `2password doctor` and the local `~/.config/2password/service-account.json` describe the configuration without opening a vault; settings contain metadata and secret references, not the token.
+
+On Codex for macOS, the filesystem sandbox can hide the saved Keychain token and block the desktop integration socket. If a sandboxed command reports “Could not read the saved service-account token,” retry the **same authorized `2password` command** through the executor's normal outside-sandbox approval path (`sandbox_permissions: "require_escalated"`). Keep the same service account, command and scope; never print the token. This retry must respect the user's credential-access authorization. Do not infer that the token is missing, expired or broken from the sandbox failure alone. A successful outside-sandbox command establishes the execution restriction; do not change the account to fix it.
+
+Do not fall back to `--desktop`, the 1Password UI, a human account or a broader vault as routine error recovery. Desktop access is a separate authority choice, used only when the user explicitly authorizes that source or an administrative operation. Run authorized desktop operations outside the sandbox too. Select the exact account ID when multiple accounts exist; `--account` is available on lookup commands and `OP_ACCOUNT` selects it for `run`/`env run`. Discover account identifiers only when needed to resolve an actual account-selection failure, not as a preflight.
+
+“No matches” means no matches in the authenticated account's visible vaults. Report that scope. It does not establish that the credential does not exist elsewhere. Adding a credential to an agent vault, widening its source permissions or granting another vault requires authority for that effect. Verify the exact source, environment and read/write scope before transferring; an item's title alone is not proof of its permissions. A read-only secret-store token can still reveal a database owner's write-capable credential; it is not proof of a SELECT-only database role. Use references and secret injection or private stdin for the transfer, and verify the destination without displaying values.
 
